### What is the Burst Balloons Problem?

Given n balloons with values, bursting balloon i yields `nums[left] * nums[i] * nums[right]` coins, where left and right are the adjacent unburst balloons. Maximize the total coins from bursting all balloons.

At National Bank of India, a liquidation desk closes positions one by one, where each closure's payout depends on its two surviving neighbors. The order determines the total proceeds.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class BurstBalloons {
    public static int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        // Pad with 1 at both ends
        int[] padded = new int[n + 2];
        padded[0] = padded[n + 1] = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) padded[i + 1] = nums[i];

        int len = padded.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[len][len];

        // Fill by increasing interval length
        for (int gap = 2; gap < len; gap++) {
            for (int i = 0; i + gap < len; i++) {
                int j = i + gap;
                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {
                    int coins = dp[i][k] + padded[i] * padded[k] * padded[j] + dp[k][j];
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i][j], coins);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[0][n + 1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balloons = {3, 1, 5, 8};
        System.out.println("Max coins: " + maxCoins(balloons));
    }
}
```

```text
Max coins: 167
```

### Why Is Choosing the Last Balloon the Key Insight?

The difficulty is that bursting a balloon changes who its neighbors are. If we reason about which balloon to burst first, the two sides stay coupled because the first burst's neighbors keep shifting. The key insight is to flip the reasoning and pick which balloon is burst last in an interval.

When balloon k is the last to burst in the open interval (i, j), every other balloon in that interval is already gone. So k's neighbors are exactly the fixed boundaries i and j. This decouples the interval into two independent subranges: (i, k) and (k, j).

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LastBalloonInsight {
    // Why LAST instead of FIRST?
    // If k is burst FIRST in (i, j):
    //   k's neighbors are i and j initially
    //   BUT after bursting k, the balloons in (i,k) and (k,j) are now
    //   adjacent to each other, coupling the two sides

    // If k is burst LAST in (i, j):
    //   All others are already gone
    //   k's neighbors are EXACTLY i and j (fixed boundaries)
    //   (i,k) and (k,j) are INDEPENDENT subproblems
}
```

### How Does the Padding Work?

We pad the original array with 1 at both ends. This ensures that when a balloon at the boundary is burst last, its multiplication includes the boundary value of 1, which does not change the product. Without padding, we would need special cases for the first and last balloons.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PaddingExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] original = {3, 1, 5, 8};
        int n = original.length;
        int[] padded = new int[n + 2];
        padded[0] = 1;          // left boundary
        padded[n + 1] = 1;      // right boundary
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) padded[i + 1] = original[i];

        System.out.print("Padded: ");
        for (int v : padded) System.out.print(v + " ");
        // Output: 1 3 1 5 8 1
    }
}
```

```text
Padded: 1 3 1 5 8 1
```

### How Does the Fill Order Work?

We fill by increasing interval length (gap between i and j). The smallest intervals (gap = 2, containing one balloon) are filled first. Larger intervals depend on smaller ones, so filling by increasing gap ensures all dependencies are ready.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class FillOrderTrace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] padded = {1, 3, 1, 5, 8, 1};
        int len = padded.length;
        int[][] dp = new int[len][len];

        for (int gap = 2; gap < len; gap++) {
            System.out.println("Gap = " + gap + ":");
            for (int i = 0; i + gap < len; i++) {
                int j = i + gap;
                for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {
                    int coins = dp[i][k] + padded[i] * padded[k] * padded[j] + dp[k][j];
                    if (coins > dp[i][j]) {
                        dp[i][j] = coins;
                        System.out.println("  dp[" + i + "][" + j + "] = " + coins + " (last=" + k + ")");
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Answer: " + dp[0][len - 1]);
    }
}
```

