Answer: 167
```

### How Is This Similar to Matrix Chain Multiplication?

Both problems use the same "choose the last operation" reframing. In Matrix Chain Multiplication, you choose where to place the last multiplication split. In Burst Balloons, you choose which balloon to burst last. Both iterate by interval length, both have cubic time.

| Aspect | Burst Balloons | Matrix Chain Multiplication |
|---|---|---|
| Choice | Last balloon to burst | Last split point |
| Decoupling | Left and right subranges | Left and right matrix products |
| Boundaries | Padded values | Matrix dimensions |
| Time | O(n^3) | O(n^3) |
| Space | O(n^2) | O(n^2) |

