### What About the Recursive Approach?

The recursive approach with memoization defines the same dp[i][j] but computes it top-down. This can be easier to implement but may hit stack limits for very large inputs.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BurstRecursive {
    private static int[][] memo;
    private static int[] padded;

    public static int maxCoins(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        padded = new int[n + 2];
        padded[0] = padded[n + 1] = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) padded[i + 1] = nums[i];
        memo = new int[n + 2][n + 2];
        for (int[] row : memo) java.util.Arrays.fill(row, -1);
        return solve(0, n + 1);
    }

    private static int solve(int i, int j) {
        if (j - i < 2) return 0;
        if (memo[i][j] != -1) return memo[i][j];
        int best = 0;
        for (int k = i + 1; k < j; k++) {
            int coins = solve(i, k) + padded[i] * padded[k] * padded[j] + solve(k, j);
            best = Math.max(best, coins);
        }
        memo[i][j] = best;
        return best;
    }
}
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:

- Matrix Chain Multiplication: same interval DP pattern, minimize multiplication cost.
- Minimum cost to merge stones: another interval DP with a group-size constraint.
- Why not choose the first balloon? Because it couples the two sides.
- Can you print the optimal burst order? Maintain a parallel `choice` array recording which k was chosen.

